      "latency_ms": 9452.6,
      "prompt_tokens": 25,
      "completion_tokens": 1194,
      "output": "\n\nWater is a chemical substance with the molecular formula **H₂O**, consisting of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom. It is a vital component of life and one of the most abundant and unique substances on Earth. Here's a breakdown of its key characteristics:\n\n### **Chemical Structure**\n- **Molecular Composition**: Each water molecule is a **polar molecule**, with a bent shape due to the arrangement of its atoms. The oxygen atom is slightly negative, while the hydrogen atoms are slightly positive, creating a dipole moment.\n- **Hydrogen Bonding**: Water molecules form **hydrogen bonds** with each other, which give it unique properties like high surface tension, cohesion, and the ability to dissolve many substances.\n\n### **Physical Properties**\n- **States of Matter**: \n  - **Liquid**: At room temperature (25°C/77°F), water is a colorless, odorless, and tasteless liquid.\n  - **Solid**: When frozen, it becomes **ice** (at 0°C/32°F), which is less dense than liquid water, allowing it to float.\n  - **Gas**: When heated, it becomes **water vapor** (at 100°C/212°F at standard pressure).\n- **Key Properties**:\n  - **High Specific Heat**: Water absorbs and releases heat slowly, helping regulate temperature in organisms and environments.\n  - **High Heat of Vaporization**: It requires significant energy to evaporate, which is why sweating cools the body.\n  - **Cohesion and Adhesion**: Molecules stick to each other (cohesion) and to other surfaces (adhesion), enabling processes like capillary action in plants.\n\n### **Role in Nature and Life**\n- **Universal Solvent**: Water dissolves more substances than any other liquid, making it essential for transporting nutrients, chemicals, and waste in living organisms.\n- **Biological Importance**: \n  - Found in all living cells, comprising 60–90% of the human body.\n  - Crucial for photosynthesis in plants and metabolic reactions in organisms.\n- **Water Cycle**: Part of a continuous cycle involving evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and runoff, sustaining ecosystems and weather patterns.\n\n### **Unique Characteristics**\n- **Anomalous Expansion**: Water reaches its maximum density at **4°C**, causing ice to float and enabling aquatic life to survive in cold climates.\n- **pH Neutrality**: Pure water is neutral (pH 7), but it can act as both an acid and a base in chemical reactions.\n\nIn summary, water is a simple yet extraordinary molecule with properties that make it indispensable for life, Earth's climate, and countless natural processes. Its ability to exist in all three states (solid, liquid, gas) and its role as a universal solvent underpin its significance across science and daily life."
    },
